const culturalTips = [
  { icon: '🏛', title: 'Maya Civilisation Today', text: 'The Maya are not a vanished civilisation — over 6 million Maya people live in Mexico, Guatemala, and Belize today. Many speak Maya languages, practise traditional agriculture, and maintain cultural traditions. The ruins are their heritage, not just tourist sites.' },
  { icon: '🗣', title: 'Hire a Local Guide', text: 'Local guides at Chichén Itzá (MXN 600–800 per group) are often Maya descendants who know the site\'s history, astronomy, and acoustics intimately. Their knowledge transforms the visit from looking at stones to understanding a civilisation. Tip generously.' },
  { icon: '🌮', title: 'Yucatecan Cuisine', text: 'The Yucatán has its own distinct cuisine — different from the rest of Mexico. Cochinita pibil, papadzules, poc chuc, salbutes, and panuchos are regional specialities. Habanero salsa is fiery. Sopa de lima is a must. Try everything at market comedores for the most authentic versions.' },
  { icon: '💧', title: 'Cenote Reverence', text: 'Cenotes were sacred to the Maya — they considered them entrances to Xibalba, the underworld. The Sacred Cenote at Chichén Itzá received offerings of gold, jade, and human sacrifice. When you swim in a cenote, you are floating in a place of deep spiritual significance.' },
  { icon: '🤝', title: 'Community Tourism', text: 'Several cenotes and sites around Valladolid are community-run by Maya villages — Xcanche at Ek Balam is a great example. Visiting these supports local livelihoods directly rather than large corporations. Ask at your hotel which cenotes are community-managed.' },
  { icon: '🌿', title: 'Protect the Sites', text: 'Never climb structures marked as off-limits, touch carvings, or use flash photography inside temples. Cenotes are fragile ecosystems — use reef-safe sunscreen, do not touch formations, and take all rubbish with you. These sites have survived a thousand years; help them survive a thousand more.' },
];
---

